# Ninja Pot Roast (One Pot)

Makes about 6 servings: 4 for dinner, 1 for your son's night snack, 1 for your husband to take to work the next day. Sears and slow cooks in the same pot.

## Ingredients
- 4 lb chuck roast
- 1 1/2 tsp salt
- 1/2 tsp black pepper
- 1 tbsp oil (for searing)
- 1 1/2 lb baby potatoes, halved
- 6 carrots, cut into chunks
- 1 onion, quartered
- 2 1/2 cups beef broth
- 1 tbsp Worcestershire sauce
- 3 cloves garlic, minced (or 1 1/2 tsp jarred)

## Instructions
1. Season the roast with salt and pepper. Set Ninja to Sauté/Sear and sear the roast on all sides directly in the pot, about 3-4 minutes per side.
2. Add potatoes, carrots, onion, broth, Worcestershire, and garlic to the pot around the roast.
3. Switch to Slow Cook on low for 8 hours, or Pressure Cook on high for 60 minutes with natural release.
4. Let rest, then slice against the grain (no shredding needed).
5. Portion roast, vegetables, and a bit of the cooking liquid into containers. Freezes well for up to 3 months.

## Make-Ahead & Defrosting
- Safe to prep ahead **refrigerated** (not frozen): chop veggies and season the roast up to 2-3 days early, or combine everything raw in one container/bag and keep it in the fridge — then just dump it in the Ninja on cook day.
- If freezing the raw roast or the combined raw mix: it must be **fully thawed in the refrigerator** (about 24 hours per 4-5 lb) before using the **Slow Cook** function. The USDA advises against putting frozen meat straight into a slow cooker — it can sit too long in the unsafe temperature zone before the cooker heats up.
- If you're short on time, the **Pressure Cook** function can safely go straight from frozen — skip searing, add extra time (start by adding about 50% more time and checking doneness), and confirm the roast reaches 145°F with a meat thermometer before serving.

## Notes
- Reheat gently on the stovetop or microwave with a splash of the reserved liquid to keep it from drying out.
- Slicing rather than shredding makes this feel like a different meal from the pulled pork/chicken nights.
- Pack one portion in a small container for a night snack and one in a work-safe container for a next-day lunch when you divide up the leftovers.
